WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:14.310
- Good afternoon, so I'm Jacob
Kim and I'm the webmaster

00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:17.360
at the Hirshhorn Museum
and Sculpture Garden.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.720
And I primarily work with
web and digital projects

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.803
that interface with on the
public engagement side of things.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.113
Dan Michaelson is our,

00:00:28.965 --> 00:00:30.405
do you want to introduce yourself?

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:34.410
- I'm an adjunct faculty in
the Graphic Design department

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.480
at the school of art.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.010
I teach a couple of the
interaction design classes

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.940
to the grad students there.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.800
But I also run a digital
studio in New York City

00:00:45.621 --> 00:00:46.454
as my full-time job.

00:00:46.454 --> 00:00:49.793
And that's how I got
connected with the Hirshhorn.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:55.330
- Right and Dan was our creative
lead and development lead

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.150
on the Hirshhorn Eye project.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:01.500
And I'll talk a little bit
about the steps that we took

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.220
up to developing this
digital engagement project

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.530
and then Dan can go into
a little bit more detail.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:12.860
So the Hirshhorn Museum is
Smithsonian's national museum

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.421
of modern and contemporary art.

00:01:15.421 --> 00:01:19.990
And our use case for machine vision was

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.740
to create a digital engagement product

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.503
to be used in our galleries.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.740
One of the benefits of being
a contemporary art museum is

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.300
that we have access to living artists.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.135
A lot of the artworks that
we exhibit in our galleries,

00:01:35.135 --> 00:01:37.690
the artists are still around today.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:40.600
So we wanted to take this
advantage and leverage it

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.520
in a way where we can share their stories

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.390
and create more context for the artworks

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.673
that are being exhibited at the Hirshhorn.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.940
When we started exploring
different engagement products

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.621
out there already in
existence and technologies,

00:01:55.621 --> 00:02:00.410
we were looking for something
that could help deliver

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.960
that point of view from
the living artists.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.860
And we started looking at a wide variety

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.430
of different technologies
and products out in existence

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.223
to help facilitate this vision.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:15.560
We looked at technology
such as VR, AR products,

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:19.214
in-gallery interactives,
native apps, and such.

00:02:19.214 --> 00:02:20.860
All of these tech products

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.990
have very strong merits of their own,

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:24.980
but they weren't exactly
what we were looking for.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.500
A lot of them were cumbersome,

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.680
they took a lot of bit of foot space

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.610
inside the museum galleries.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:32.530
And we wanted something

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.533
that would be a little bit more invisible.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:43.780
So with our market research,
we finally arrived on a

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.250
vision for a product that we
wanted to develop on our own.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:52.250
And to help part of the
vision for that product

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.780
that we wanted to develop
was to create a connection

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.960
between the visitor, the living
artists, and the artworks

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.490
that are presented in our
galleries, and tell their stories.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.500
We started with an old model

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.380
of the traditional audio
guides that are available,

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.492
and we wanted to use emergent technologies

00:03:13.492 --> 00:03:17.010
to create the next generation

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.093
of audio-guided museum
guide for our space.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.860
And when we started that
process we started looking

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.080
at technologies such as QR codes,

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.621
location-based technologies, beacons.

00:03:29.621 --> 00:03:34.060
But all of that felt very cumbersome

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:38.280
and not very user-friendly
for our visiting public.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.970
Ultimately we settled

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.100
on leveraging image-recognition technology

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:47.100
to create a product where a
user could go to a website,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.240
point their phone at
artworks, and listen directly

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.490
from the artist talking
about their practice.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.730
So in partnership with Linked by Air

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.013
and Antenna International,

00:04:00.013 --> 00:04:03.550
we developed this product
called the Hirshhorn Eye.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:07.040
The Hirshhorn Eye is a
web-based museum guide.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.530
It uses computer vision
to recognize the artworks

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.367
in our galleries and deliver content.

00:04:13.367 --> 00:04:16.563
And I'd like to show you a
little video about how it works.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:27.901
- [Dan] It just takes
a minute to start once.

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:32.901
- There we go. (soft music playing)

00:04:46.733 --> 00:04:50.570
- Welcome to my artwork in
the lobby of the Hirshhorn.

00:05:01.286 --> 00:05:02.960
- So I thought I'd talk a little bit

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:07.960
about the process that let
this project come together.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:13.040
And maybe it's nice to end or conclude

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:18.030
this amazing set of talks
with some snapshots in a way,

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:23.030
of a team trying to apply
the kind of research

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.370
that you guys are doing

00:05:26.431 --> 00:05:28.013
and that everyone here is
doing in different ways.

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:33.110
One thing that we realized early on is

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:36.710
that thinking cross-functionally

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:40.560
and in an interdisciplinary
way is really essential.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.260
And so I'll start with the thank yous,

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.510
rather than end with them.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:47.770
The project was a collaboration

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:51.440
between actually a pretty
wide group of stakeholders

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:52.630
within the Hirshhorn museum,

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:56.160
from the director, to curators,
to exhibition designers,

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.160
and Jacob, and visitor experience
people, as well as the CIO

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.870
on technical infrastructure,
issues that arose,

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.880
Antenna International, which
helped with production,

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:15.880
really great team of filmmakers

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:18.300
in New York called Captain and the Fox,

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.973
a user testing specialist, a marketer.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:26.030
The image recognition itself is

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:28.050
the easy part in this project.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:33.050
It's an API service from a
company based in Barcelona.

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:38.110
And to Catherine's point,
the actual image recognition,

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:41.450
at least in a project
like this, is a commodity.

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:43.960
Basically we're a small business

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:48.960
and the technical challenges,
per se, were not large for us.

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:55.360
We didn't need to be an Amazon.

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:59.270
And similarly, my students in
the art school are doing lots

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:02.710
of stuff around computer
vision, image recognition now.

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:07.710
So there are, this kind of
work is definitely percolating

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.680
into the creative atmosphere for sure.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:14.270
And I think that that's
really leading lots

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.580
of really interesting opportunities.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:20.040
Our role was the user
experience of the product

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:21.797
and really thinking that
through holistically,

00:07:21.797 --> 00:07:24.570
the interface design,
what does it look like

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.293
and how does it actually
work on the screen,

00:07:27.293 --> 00:07:30.840
and the engineering other than
the actual image recognition

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:35.010
neural network itself.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.070
Also really involved were the artists

00:07:38.509 --> 00:07:40.390
who you saw, Olafur Eliasson starting

00:07:41.346 --> 00:07:42.960
to tell a story about his work.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.960
But the key to this was
not, it's not interesting

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:52.840
to tell the user what is
the title of the work.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.960
The purpose of this is to connect the user

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.767
to an artist talking about the work,

00:07:57.767 --> 00:07:59.733
so what stories they're telling,

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:05.197
is like 90% of the product.

00:08:05.197 --> 00:08:08.870
And also, as I'll talk
about, we learned a lot

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:12.030
from what we would typically call users,

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:13.940
but a museum would tend to call visitors,

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:15.670
the people who are actually using

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:17.020
this product in this space.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:21.640
So today Rebecca really, at the
very beginning this morning,

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:24.300
talked about or evoked a large team

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.730
in big science working together.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:30.500
And in a humanities project like this one,

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:33.223
an interdisciplinary team
is also really important.

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:39.250
Especially for a project that takes images

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:41.110
in the physical environment as its input

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:43.270
and basically it's images in, images out.

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:46.320
You get other moving images as the output

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.680
and you're transforming
pixels into pixels.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.410
So thinking synthetically
about user experience,

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:55.850
technology, visitors, artists, curation,

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:57.740
what are the sequence of works

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.240
that are being brought together?

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.090
Content production, staff maintainers,

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:05.940
how are people at the Hirshhorn
authoring these stories

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:08.380
and continuing to expand and change them

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.455
as new exhibitions are mounted?

00:09:10.455 --> 00:09:14.310
And also how are visitor experienced staff

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:18.435
on the floor helping visitors use this?

00:09:18.435 --> 00:09:20.330
And also the physical environment,

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:23.160
which I'll talk a lot about,
or talk more about in a minute.

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:26.082
Thinking synthetically and
holistically about all of that,

00:09:26.082 --> 00:09:27.920
even when those are the domains

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.610
of multiple different vendors,

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:33.880
multiple different departments,
multiple kinds of thinking,

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:35.837
that's really important for success.

00:09:35.837 --> 00:09:40.837
Here's an early snapshot
of a team of Hirshhorn's

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:43.140
and Smithsonian staff
and also that's Alice

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:46.750
from Antenna brainstorming and testing.

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:51.280
Lots of this project was
scanning simulations of images

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.673
in small offices and figuring
what that would be like.

00:09:53.673 --> 00:09:58.673
I think this was one of the
first web-based applications

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:00.940
of its kind in this space.

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:05.033
And this is possible, or
that is the case because,

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:12.220
as of 2017 when it was only
with iOS 11 was it possible

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:18.510
to scan an image in real
time in a web browser.

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:22.090
So iOS 11 came out in the fall of 2017,

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:25.300
right around the time that this
product was meant to launch.

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:29.500
So we were developing towards
a thing that didn't yet exist.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:34.200
And we really didn't want to
launch this as a native app,

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:37.530
because we know from
research that the take-up

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:41.799
of native apps in a museum
like this is really low.

00:10:41.799 --> 00:10:43.240
People don't want to install
an app on their device

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:46.553
that they're only gonna
use once or not often.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:52.180
So that involves a sort of
embrace of risk a little bit

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:55.280
on everybody's part to think
about developing for something

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.030
that hopefully was gonna work
six months down the road.

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:02.449
And we managed that by having fallbacks.

00:11:02.449 --> 00:11:04.730
We had a certain native app fallback

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:06.273
that we could use if necessary.

00:11:07.121 --> 00:11:12.121
Peter talked about, or
mentioned, how to work with,

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:16.600
rather than against, human
curation and expert description.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:20.370
And I think that was
really key to our thinking

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:24.040
that it's not the
technology that's the end,

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:25.970
but the technology is just an instrument

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:29.270
for story-telling and for connecting users

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:31.593
to living artists telling their stories.

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:38.283
User testing was a huge
part of our project.

00:11:40.294 --> 00:11:44.150
And we did that with Hirshhorn staff,

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:47.300
we did it with Smithsonian
staff at one point,

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:49.830
like hundreds of Smithsonian staff.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:53.340
We did it occasionally with
random visitors on the floor.

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:57.854
And very importantly, we
did it by recruiting testers

00:11:57.854 --> 00:12:02.030
on Facebook and places like
that in order to ensure

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:07.030
that we had a diverse
demographics among our testers.

00:12:09.507 --> 00:12:14.507
The big goal of the project
was to show diverse artists

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:21.970
actually from of all genders,
nationalities, ethnicities,

00:12:22.481 --> 00:12:27.481
and similarly a goal was
to make contemporary art,

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:32.860
which can be alienating and
intimidating, feel accessible

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:36.150
to lots of different kinds of visitors.

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:40.449
Meaning tourists off the
mall who were not art experts

00:12:40.449 --> 00:12:43.973
and also people of all ages
and cultural backgrounds.

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:50.110
So testing with lots of different kinds

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:53.130
of visitors was essential and we learned

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:54.840
so much from doing that.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:56.810
And I'll talk about some of the,

00:12:56.810 --> 00:12:58.853
I'll give a couple examples
of what we learned.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:03.880
Testing at the Hirshhorn
goes outside as well.

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:05.573
It is also a sculpture garden.

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:09.883
Here are non-staff testers.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:13.824
And even seeing how people
physically bodily use

00:13:13.824 --> 00:13:18.824
this product or how do they stand,

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:20.460
how much are they looking at artwork,

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:23.883
how much are they looking at
their phone is really helpful.

00:13:24.795 --> 00:13:29.795
Both quantitatively, like
sometimes it's something we needed

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:32.960
to address or remediate, like
when people were not using

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:34.810
the thing in a way that was gonna work

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.150
or would interfere with their enjoyment.

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:40.220
But also qualitatively, just understanding

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:42.630
that different people are gonna use

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:44.280
this interface in different ways.

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:54.535
One thing we learned pretty quickly,

00:13:54.535 --> 00:13:57.280
not so much from user
testing but from looking

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:02.280
at other similar applications
is that lots of people

00:14:04.315 --> 00:14:06.040
would be confused and think
that you can use this website

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:07.950
when you're not at the Hirshhorn Museum.

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:12.844
And so we thought should
we use location permission?

00:14:12.844 --> 00:14:15.260
But that can be kind of intrusive.

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.670
And I think the team kind of
finally collaboratively came up

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:19.910
with this really basic,

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:23.000
this is the dumbest thing
you've seen all day.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:25.280
We'll just ask people
and that's actually been,

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:26.810
I think a really effective solution

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:27.700
and something that's really,

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:31.500
we design lots of websites for museums

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:33.660
and this simple way of thinking

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:35.540
has actually really been
influencing how I think

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:38.003
about other museum websites as well.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:49.500
Early designs, when you
could sort of keep scanning.

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:52.910
So you could almost collect artworks.

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:56.650
And because the scanner
is so sensitive in a way

00:14:56.650 --> 00:14:58.913
and artworks can be hung close together,

00:15:00.176 --> 00:15:02.037
the idea was that we wouldn't wanna,

00:15:02.037 --> 00:15:03.729
you could be confused
by accidentally having

00:15:03.729 --> 00:15:05.240
the wrong thing triggered.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:06.160
You're trying to scan one thing,

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.030
but there's another
thing right next to it.

00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:11.720
And so we thought even while
you're watching one artist talk

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:14.670
to you it might keep
scanning and then collect

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:16.450
up in this tray at the top.

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:20.290
And that's the prototype
that we tested with users

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:21.870
and we quickly found
that that was confusing.

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:24.010
And we needed to take this
already simple interface

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:26.743
and simplify it even further
and remove that feature.

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:35.720
Sort of physical surprise that we saw was

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:41.430
that visitors really wanna scan the label.

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:44.750
They're so trained to be
scanning like a QR code,

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:46.903
and also to be searching for a label,

00:15:46.903 --> 00:15:50.410
that they would walk up to the label

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:51.840
and get really close to it and scan it

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:54.630
and then step back and
look at the artwork.

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:59.630
And what our goal was that
you wouldn't need to be so

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:03.930
didactic in your use of it.

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:06.330
But that it could be a
kind of freer experience

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:07.580
of walking around and just kind

00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:10.500
of breezing your phone around
and things would pop up.

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:15.160
And we knew from testing with our visitors

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:18.310
that no one will read the instruction.

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:20.070
And that's not a judgment,
that's just for sure.

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:21.818
I wouldn't either, you're
trying to get to it,

00:16:21.818 --> 00:16:23.890
get to the experience.

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:28.570
And so we tried this
illustration that could model

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:33.133
the behavior that we were
hoping for visitors to take.

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:39.740
Even so, it was so fascinating
walking around with visitors

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:42.260
and talking to them about
their experience using this.

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:44.450
And a lot of them talked to us

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:48.230
about their consciousness
of wanting to look up

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:51.970
at the work and not get too
absorbed in their phone.

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:54.110
Some visitors just hate it

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:57.530
and they absolutely don't
wanna use an electronic device

00:16:57.530 --> 00:16:58.780
when they're in a museum.

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:05.250
Other, some kinds of young
people they just wanna,

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:07.320
they walk through, they
scan absolutely everything,

00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:09.820
they watch the first three
seconds of every video.

00:17:13.211 --> 00:17:18.100
And other people really had
a transformative experience

00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:21.610
with it and talked about seeing artists

00:17:21.610 --> 00:17:22.610
who looked like them.

00:17:22.610 --> 00:17:27.610
Understanding for the first
time that a contemporary art

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:33.770
that might be ugly to them,
understanding the motivations

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:36.360
of an artist and where it
comes from really deepened

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:37.620
their connection to it in a way

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:40.200
that sometimes they hadn't had before.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:42.430
But this whole genre of our...

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:51.300
This product is used in
real life and I would argue

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:54.150
that that's true of
everything on some level,

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:56.672
of everything that was talked about today,

00:17:56.672 --> 00:17:59.320
that there's a physical context

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:04.320
in which people are reading
books, or doing data entry,

00:18:06.290 --> 00:18:09.460
or learning about art,
or engaging with art.

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:14.460
And that physical disposition
was a big influence

00:18:16.420 --> 00:18:18.350
on the design of the interface as well as

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:20.448
on solving some of the kinds of problems

00:18:20.448 --> 00:18:23.263
that I've just alluded to.

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:27.600
So early on when we were
doing testing it was important

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:31.210
to also include the labels
and think about the context

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:32.940
that this is happening in.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:37.940
This is not a sort of
disembodied codex of jpgs.

00:18:45.860 --> 00:18:49.195
For the sort of fallbacks,
so that a user who,

00:18:49.195 --> 00:18:54.100
the image recognition does not
work very well on label text,

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:57.380
it's not distinct enough and so,

00:18:57.380 --> 00:18:59.330
but it was really important
that there be a fallback

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:00.820
for someone who would scan the label.

00:19:00.820 --> 00:19:03.750
We learned that from watching visitors

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:05.440
and also because some of these works,

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:07.415
which I'll talk about in a minute,

00:19:07.415 --> 00:19:08.560
are very hard to recognize.

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:12.715
And so in those cases, well
in every case basically,

00:19:12.715 --> 00:19:16.220
there's an image, a frame
of the video that you get,

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:19.167
for example, which is
reliably recognizable.

00:19:20.309 --> 00:19:23.250
And which also cues the
user that cues the visitor,

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:26.350
that this work is in the system.

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:29.340
And one of the nice things
about having such a low barrier

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:31.380
to entry that is not a
native application is

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:33.350
that there is this short
url that you can just enter

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:34.710
into your web browser at any time

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:36.560
so that you can start anywhere

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:39.290
in the system and follow any pathway.

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:42.900
But basically every work in
the museum is in the system

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:45.813
with a few exceptions at this point.

00:19:49.548 --> 00:19:53.831
And finally, letting visitors
know that this is here

00:19:53.831 --> 00:19:57.050
and that there is this
additional available layer

00:19:57.050 --> 00:19:59.430
to the experiences is really sort of one

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:01.653
of the biggest challenges.

00:20:06.296 --> 00:20:07.659
And it's even more heightened actually

00:20:07.659 --> 00:20:10.930
at the Smithsonian museums on the mall

00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:15.930
where there's no pay gate,
there's no ticketing.

00:20:16.390 --> 00:20:18.390
So you really just walk
right into the museum.

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:22.740
So there's no point at which,
there's no inherent point

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:24.947
at which a visitor services staff can say,

00:20:24.947 --> 00:20:26.797
"Here is this thing that you can do."

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:31.980
So on the one hand, there's
this low barrier to entry.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:33.140
On the other hand, we really need people

00:20:33.140 --> 00:20:35.123
to know that they can take that step.

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:41.030
The exhibition that we launched with,

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:45.282
ironically was called what
absence is made out of.

00:20:45.282 --> 00:20:50.282
And an image recognition
app is about the pixels.

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:52.880
It's a kind of a Ghost in the Shell.

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:56.280
It is about dematerialized objects.

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:58.820
So what absence is made of

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:01.688
was a very poetic
exhibition to start with.

00:21:01.688 --> 00:21:06.688
It featured not exclusively
minimalist and conceptual art,

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:09.483
but a lot of minimalist
and conceptual art.

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:13.200
Reading from the description
of the exhibition,

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:15.740
a curator described it
as the spaces left behind

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:17.540
when something disappears

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:20.010
and as I work on a massive scale,

00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:22.180
yet with the barest materials.

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:27.180
And so this is Didier
Vermeiren's sculpture.

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:29.600
It's meant, in part,

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:32.983
to look like the pedestal
for other artwork.

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:39.540
That's Fred Sandback, it's string.

00:21:40.430 --> 00:21:43.300
That's Reynier Novo's black squares.

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:45.700
And these are Joseph Beuys' black squares.

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:50.700
And it turns out that once
you've seen even one black square

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:54.260
into your image recognition database,

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:58.043
every dark corner of the museum
now pops up Joseph Beuys.

00:22:01.133 --> 00:22:04.620
So Peter kind of talked
about an unexpected,

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:08.797
that Fred Wilson image and talk

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:11.110
about unexpected juxtapositions,

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:15.527
and really created a
situation in which work

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:21.630
that was meant to be curated together

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:24.310
and tell a really amazing
story in fact matched up

00:22:24.310 --> 00:22:26.460
with each other through
this technological prism

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:31.013
in a way that was a challenge
as well as an opportunity.

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:34.673
That's Giovanni Anselmo.

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:37.410
This hand is not part of the artwork.

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:42.410
The artwork is just a single
slide in a slide projector

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:45.727
with the word visible
projecting into empty space.

00:22:45.727 --> 00:22:47.060
And so you can only see it

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:50.030
when you interrupt the projection.

00:22:50.030 --> 00:22:51.870
And it's also a single slide,

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:55.570
so it just slowly burns
actually in the light.

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:58.230
As it changes it get more
and more brown every day&gt;

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:03.230
so this is an artwork that is
meant to defy machine vision.

00:23:07.030 --> 00:23:11.620
But we can work around this problems often

00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:14.440
by seeding the scene.

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:16.530
And by, as a couple of
people have talked about,

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:18.960
by making sure that there
we're including humans

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:20.730
in this process of thinking about

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:26.352
where the artwork is located,
literally in this case.

00:23:26.352 --> 00:23:28.940
And taking a couple steps back

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:31.130
or moving around the
sculpture to see images

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:32.610
that are gonna be effective.

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:35.499
And that means again watching visitors

00:23:35.499 --> 00:23:37.623
and doing lots of testing.

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:40.400
And I think that's a kind of,

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:46.300
like Steven was talking about,

00:23:46.300 --> 00:23:50.220
a kind of human knowledge
that it is always going

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:52.660
to be an essential part of
these projects especially.

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:54.827
And will be most effective when you work

00:23:54.827 --> 00:23:56.913
in an interdisciplinary way.

00:23:58.241 --> 00:24:01.300
So the last thing was I
wanted to show is the solution

00:24:01.300 --> 00:24:04.280
to the Anselmo problem and
critics gotta talk about

00:24:04.280 --> 00:24:06.670
in a work like that, is it
the artwork, the projection,

00:24:06.670 --> 00:24:09.023
is it the situation, is
it the slide projector.

00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:11.390
In our case, it's the slide projector

00:24:11.390 --> 00:24:13.453
that scans actually really effectively.

00:24:15.517 --> 00:24:18.761
So here's that work shining back out

00:24:18.761 --> 00:24:22.600
and this is a team of
testers from the Hirshhorn

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:25.600
and the broader Smithsonian, kinda' trying

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:26.850
to figure this stuff out.

00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:30.063
So I'll stop there, thanks a lot.

00:24:32.418 --> 00:24:35.418
(audience clapping)

00:24:38.752 --> 00:24:41.130
- And I just wanted to add that,

00:24:41.130 --> 00:24:43.530
going into the next section
of the talk is that,

00:24:44.490 --> 00:24:46.090
while the Hirshhorn Eye was driven

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:48.540
with a very specific content
strategy at the Hirshhorn.

00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:52.090
We believe that what we
created is applicable widely

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:55.470
across different subject matter.

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:57.830
So what we've done at the Smithsonian is

00:24:57.830 --> 00:25:00.370
that we've started
taking the Hirshhorn Eye

00:25:00.370 --> 00:25:04.300
and adapting it for use
across our different museums.

00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:06.290
And we're also working

00:25:06.290 --> 00:25:09.590
with third-party external organizations

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:13.010
and trying to adapt the Hirshhorn
Eye for their use as well.

00:25:13.010 --> 00:25:14.890
Eventually what we hope to do is

00:25:14.890 --> 00:25:17.760
to create a open source
version of the Hirshhorn Eye

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:20.460
and to make it readily
available for smaller museums

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:21.700
and organizations to pick it up

00:25:21.700 --> 00:25:24.330
and deploy it at their space, as well.

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:25.680
Thank you.
- [Man] Thanks.

00:25:26.884 --> 00:25:29.884
(audience clapping)

00:25:31.971 --> 00:25:35.054
(soft music playing)

